Release 1.1.5 Windows package validation correction.

Root cause:
- The installed executable validator still compared the extracted Windows icon to the pixel hash of the previous Beaver icon.
- The v1.1.5 NSIS package correctly embedded the new icon, so post-install validation failed after the package itself built successfully.

Correction:
- src-tauri/icons/icon.ico is now the single packaging authority for Windows branding.
- check-nsis-migration.ps1 passes that source asset to the installed executable validator.
- windows-artifact-helpers.ps1 extracts and hashes pixels from both the installed executable icon and the authoritative ICO, with bounded files and fail-closed error handling.
- The obsolete hardcoded old-icon pixel hash was removed.
- Brand and migration tests lock the approved ICO and the complete source-to-validator wiring.

Verification:
- npm run test:package-migration: 6/6 passed.
- npx vitest run src/components/ui/__tests__/brand-assets.test.ts: 11/11 passed.
- npm run test:release-workflow: 39/39 passed.
- npm run test:install: passed.
- npm run test:brand-boundaries: 14/14 passed.
- npm run check: 7/7 green; 2499 frontend/script tests and 3913 Rust tests passed, 5 ignored.
- graphify update . completed.

Scope:
- Only the five release/brand validation files in this commit are part of the correction.
- Unrelated Beaver site mockup and screenshot working-tree changes were preserved.
